 { Many different factors play a role in choosing which piece exchange to go for, if any. Over the course of this section, we'll show examples that bring into play other positional elements. These include space, piece activity, king safety, pawn breaks, as well as the pawn structure more generally. Let's begin with a first, common, type of situation. } 1. { -KEY- } Nb1 $1 { The knight on d2 was not well-placed because of Black's space advantage in the centre. Importantly, the knight now eyes the a3 square. } Rfe8 ( { Black could not have avoided the bishop exchange since after } 1... Bc7 2. Ba3 { if the queen moves, the rook on f8 will be lost. } ) 
2. Ba3 $1 { White forces the exchange of dark-squared bishops. } Bxa3 ( 2... c5 { Technically, Black could keep the bishops on the board with this move, but now they would lose their grip on the centre so White would be very happy after } 3. Nc3 $14 { with a slight edge, due to the well-placed pieces and central pressure. } ) 
3. Rxa3 $11 { A positional success! White has exchanged their passive bishop for Black's best minor piece. In this game, the key lay in understanding the effect that Black's central space advantage was having on each side's minor pieces. Let's dive into some other piece exchange positions and take a look at what elements are in play there. } 
