 { This position was reached in an online game between Magnus Carlsen and GM Sergei Zhigalko. } ( { White to play. Do you think it's best to play } 1. Nxe7 { or } 1. Nxf6 $2 ) 
1. { -KEY- } Nxe7+ $1 { While it's nice to pick up the bishop pair, the main advantage of this move is that now Black has been weakened on the dark squares. This sets up a strong plan of advancing the kingside pawns. } ( 1. Nxf6 $2 Bxf6 2. g4 h6 $17 { Black easily sets up a bind along the dark squares, stopping the attack in its tracks. } ) 
Qxe7 2. g4 $1 Nd7 ( 2... h6 $6 { Here this move fails to stop the advance, when after } 3. g5 hxg5 4. Bxg5 $16 { the pin is very difficult to break while Black's king is already feeling very unsafe. } ) 
3. g5 { Threatening the f5-f6 pawn break. } f6 4. h4 $14 { Black has stopped the attack as best he can, but the kingside pressure will remain for a long time. In this example, the main reason for exchanging pieces on e7 was to set up a kingside pawn storm and pawn breaks. } 
