 { This position is taken from a game where Magnus again has the White pieces, with his opponent being IM Rudik Makarian. } ( { Do you think White should play } 1. Nxc7 { , or build up the pressure on the d-file with } 1. Rd1 { instead? } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Nxc7 $1 { The best move! The bishop may have been passive, but it was the key defender of the weak pawns on a5 and d6. This is why Magnus was happy to trade off his powerful central knight. } ( 1. Rd1 $6 Bxd5 $1 { Breaking the rules! Normally, Black would want to keep the light-squared bishop and lose the dark-squared one, but in this case other factors are more important. } 2. Rxd5 Qh4 3. Rd3 Ng5 4. Bxg5 Qxg5 $13 { As the chess saying goes, 'the bad bishop defends the good pawns!' Black is thankful to have kept his passive dark-squared bishop, since it is the key defender of the vulnerable d6 and a5 pawns. White's position remains better, but it won't be easy to break through. } ) 
Qxc7 2. Bc3 { Preparing Qd2 next, with a double attack against a5 and d6. } Nf6 ( { It was already too late to try to defend the weaknesses, since after } 2... Ra8 3. Qd2 Rfd8 4. Rd1 $18 { The d6 pawn will fall on the next move. } ) 
3. Qd2 Ra8 4. Rxd6 { White is winning, and the game continued for only two more moves. } Rfd8 5. Rxd8+ Rxd8 6. Bxa5 $18 ( { Snapping up a second pawn since after } 6. Bxa5 Rxd2 { there is } 7. Bxc7 { . In a hopeless position, Black resigned. The main elements here were common pawn types (isolated, backward) and the presence of a semi-open d-file. So far we have seen two examples of Magnus exchanging dark-squared bishops. Sometimes however, the opposite holds true and you may wish to avoid a piece exchange in order to benefit your pawns. Let's take a look at an example of this next. } ) 

