 { Once again we have a position played by Magnus Carlsen, against GM Zhigalko. White to play finds the only path to an advantage with his next move. } 1. { -KEY- } e5 $1 { Keeping the bishops on the board is critical to success on the kingside. } ( 1. Bxf6 $2 Nxf6 { The exchange of bishops leaves easy entry points on the dark squares for Black. } 2. e5 Nd7 3. g4 { Going for the same plan as in the game. } Qb4 $1 4. Rf1 Qc3 $13 { Black's queenside pressure is at least as dangerous as any kingside attack that White might be trying to stir up. } ) 
Be7 2. g4 $1 { Having avoided the exchange of pieces White can safely continue his attack, since the bishop on c3 does a great job of guarding against counterplay while continuing to pressure the weak pawn on a5. } Qa6 3. Rf1 Nb6 4. f5 $1 { Breaking through. } exf5 5. gxf5 c4 { Black desperately tries to stir up some counterplay. } 6. bxc4 dxc4 7. dxc4 Nd7 8. e6 $1 { A second pawn break on the kingside strips the Black king of his final line of defense. } Nc5 9. exf7+ Kxf7 10. Qh5+ Kg8 11. f6 $1 { The third and final pawn break. } Bxf6 12. Bxf6 gxf6 { From here Magnus erred in time trouble, though he did get the win. The cleanest way to finish the game was: } 13. Rxf6 $18 ( { since } 13. Rxf6 Rxd2 $2 { is not possible due to } 14. Qxe8 Kg7 15. Qf8 { . The key to making the right decision was spotting that the queenside pawns were arranged on light squares. By keeping the dark-squared bishop, White thwarted any chances of counterplay, allowing him to safely build up an attack on the kingside. } ) 

