 { Oftentimes, a piece exchange will force a change of structure. The right decision will depend on the answer to two questions: (1) Will the exchange improve my (or my opponent's) structure? (2) Will the exchange improve my (or my opponent's) piece activity? } 1. { -KEY- } Nxe5 $1 { By exchanging, Black is forced into accepting a worse structure. } ( 1. Bb3 $2 Nxf3 $1 { Black is happy to exchange on f3. } 2. Qxf3 Be6 $11 { Black has easily equalised, with active pieces and the a1-h8 diagonal staying open. } ) 
dxe5 { The result of forcing the locked pawns on e4/e5 is that Black's dark-squared bishop is shut in behind its own pawns. Both of White's bishops on the other hand are quite well-placed. } 2. Qxd8 Rxd8 3. Bb3 $16 { With the centre locked, White has the superior minor pieces. They enjoy a risk-free advantage, with the opportunity to expand on both the kingside and queenside. This type of exchange where the goal is to shut out a piece is very common. As we saw in this example, bishops make for particularly good targets since the second a pawn stands in its way it can go from a very active piece to a complete bystander. Let's turn to a more advanced version of this next. } 
