 ( { Which move do you think is best between } 1. Bb3 Nxd2 { and } exd5 $2 ) 
 { -KEY- } Nxd2 $1 { The only way for Black to avoid serious trouble! } ( 1... exd5 $2 { In the game, Black played this move and their position quickly fell apart. } 2. Nxe4 $1 { White takes advantage of the opportunity to change the structure. } dxe4 3. Bc4 $1 { The first benefit of the exchange is apparent, now White's c4 bishop becomes incredibly active. } Qd7 4. e6 $1 { The active bishop on c4 supports the pawn break, and at the same time brings the b2 bishop to life. } fxe6 5. Qg4 $18 { Black resigned as he had no defense against the dual threats of 18.Qxg7# and 18.Bxe6+. } ) 
2. Qxd2 Qxd5 { Threatening mate on g2. } 3. e4 { The pressure against g2 forces White to shut down the h1-a8 diagonal. Black is happy to force this since now White's bishop on d3 has lost a lot of its activity. } Qc6 $15 { The dust has settled, and Black is left with the better practical chances. The reason is that their 3 vs 2 pawn majority on the queenside is easier to mobilise than White's majority on the opposite flank. In this example and the previous one, we saw the intimate link between a change in structure and piece activity. Sometimes however, the purpose behind a piece exchange will be to damage the structure itself. Let's take a look at this for the final two examples of the section. } 
