 { We arrive at a position seen in the game Wolf,H - Alekhine,A (1923) . } 16... { -KEY- } Bxd3 $1 { Alekhine spots a tactic that allows him to collapse White's pawn structure. } ( 16... Rxe2 $17 { would still have kept some advantage due to other factors like greater space and a healthier structure, but the text move is much stronger. } ) 
17. cxd3 { Unfortunately for White, since the alternative moves are not without their problems, Heinrich Wolf grudgingly recaptures with the pawn. Not only is the structure very ugly, but more importantly this pawn formation has locked his dark-squared bishop out of the game. } ( 17. Qxd3 $2 { This is the desired move, but it fails to } Rxe2 18. Qxe2 d3 $19 { winning a piece. } ) 
 ( 17. Rxe8 { also fails. } Bxf1 $1 18. Rxf8 Kxf8 19. Kxf1 Bxd2 $19 { Black is a clean pawn to the good. } ) 
Rxe2 18. Qxe2 { The piece exchanges have left White with unenviable triple pawns along the d-file, while his dark-squared bishop is locked out of the action. } Qf5 $1 { Winning a pawn immediately. } 19. Rf2 Qxd5 { Initially I was going to close out the game here. However, in case you may be curious to witness the fate of White's trapped bishop over the course of the remainder of the game, you can find the rest of the moves unannotated: } 20. Qe4 Qe6 21. f5 Qe5 22. Qxe5 dxe5 23. Kg2 f6 24. Kf3 Bd8 25. Ke4 Be7 26. Rf1 Rd8 27. Rc1 a5 28. g4 Kf7 29. Ba3 b6 30. h4 g6 31. Rf1 h5 32. fxg6+ Kxg6 33. gxh5+ Kf7 34. Rg1 Ke6 35. h6 Rh8 36. Rg6 Bf8 { White resigned. The permanent passivity of the bishop proved fatal for White ever since the forced recapture with 17.cxd3. Having pieces temporarily out of the game is common, and usually survivable. However, as we discussed in the section on Activity & Passivity, it is very rare to be able to stay alive if any piece is permanently shut out of the game. } 
