 ( 12... Bf8 12. Nf3 { leaves White with an edge, but there is an even stronger move here. Can you find it? } ) 
13. { -KEY- } Qf3 $1 { A clever move. No matter which way Black responds, he will be left with a damaged pawn structure. } Nf6 ( 13... hxg5 { This move is similarly problematic. } 14. Bxd5 O-O 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. Nxe5 $16 { White is a pawn to the good with the better structure, while Black's bishop pair does not offer enough compensation. } ) 
14. Bxc6+ $1 bxc6 15. Ne4 $16 { White was much better in the game Dolmatov,S - Kortschnoj,V (1999). In this case, the use of simple tactics allowed him to force piece exchanges that would fracture the Black pawn structure. This brings us to a close for this section. Good judgment when it comes to piece exchanges is about having a broad understanding of different positional elements. Sometimes an exchange will help you carve out a space advantage. Other times it may be to set up a pawn break or to attack the king. On other occasions it may be about inflicting or avoiding structural damage. The reasons are varied, and so the best way of improving this area of your game is to focus on developing your positional understanding as a whole. Next up, we'll move on to a closely connected section: minor piece battles! } 
